# Registry binding every field of `AnonymousInstallPayload` to the words that
# disclose it to the user.
#
# The switch defaults ON. That is only defensible because the app states what it
# sends before it sends anything -- so the disclosure is not documentation, it is
# the consent basis, and a field that no disclosure mentions is a field being
# collected without consent.
#
# Nothing mechanical connected the two until now. v0.76 added three fields, and
# BOTH disclosure surfaces still said "two things": the first-run card and
# Settings > Privacy, hardcoded separately, saying the same wrong thing twice.
# This repo has already paid for that shape once -- one predicate, two call
# sites, only the UI one fixed (#468/#475).
#
# Format -- three pipe-separated fields:
#   <CodingKeys case> | <phrase that must appear in EVERY disclosure surface> | <what it is>
#
# The phrase is matched after whitespace collapsing, so a line-wrapped Swift
# string literal still matches. Keep phrases short and quotable; a long one just
# breaks on the next copy edit without catching anything more.
#
# Exempt by name in the checker, not here: `installID`, which every surface
# describes as "the id is random and is deleted when you uninstall" rather than
# by parameter name.

channel           | installed                | which distribution channel this copy came from
appVersion        | installed                | marketing version only, no build number
osVersion         | installed                | major.minor, coarsened on the client
providerDetected  | found a                  | whether it ever found a CLI to track
helperConnected   | helper connected         | whether the local helper answered over its socket
costShown         | cost to show             | whether the app ever had a non-zero cost for today
uiLanguage        | language it is displaying | which .lproj catalogue the app resolved
